---@class zendesk.CreateOrganizationMembershipRequest
---@field organization_id? number The ID of the organization to assign the user to, supplied at the root of the request instead of inside `organization_membership`. It is only consulted when no `user_id` is given at the root or in the query string; when it is consulted, `organization_membership.organization_id` is ignored. This may equivalently be sent as an `organization_id` **query parameter**, which takes precedence over the root of the body. An organization must likewise be identified in **at least one of** three places — this property, the `organization_id` query parameter, or `organization_membership.organization_id` — and a request that names none of them is a 422. As with `user_id`, that rule is stated rather than encoded, because the query location is not visible to a request-body schema.
---@field organization_membership zendesk.OrganizationMembershipInput
---@field user_id? number The ID of the user to assign to the organization, supplied at the root of the request instead of inside `organization_membership`. When present it selects the user the membership is created for, and `organization_membership.user_id` is ignored. This may equivalently be sent as a `user_id` **query parameter**, which takes precedence over the root of the body. A user must be identified in **at least one of** three places — this property, the `user_id` query parameter, or `organization_membership.user_id` — and a request that names none of them is a 422. That rule is stated here rather than encoded as a schema constraint, deliberately: one of the three legal locations is the **query string**, which lies outside the request body a body schema can see. Any structural encoding of "at least one of" would therefore have to reject a body that names the user nowhere — even though such a body is a legal `201` whenever the `user_id` query parameter supplies it. Being unenforced is the lesser fault: over-strictness here would forbid calls the API accepts.

