---@class zendesk.CustomObjectRecordResponse
---@field custom_object_record? zendesk.CustomObjectRecord

---The writable attributes accepted when updating a custom object record. Every property is optional and an omitted property is left
---unchanged. `name` and `external_id` fall back to the record's current value, so they cannot be cleared by sending null — send a new
---value instead
---@class zendesk.CustomObjectRecordUpdateInput
---@field custom_object_fields? zendesk.CustomObjectRecordUpdateInputCustomObjectFieldsMap|distilled.Null A map of the object's custom field keys to their new values. Merged into the record: only the keys present in the map are written and any field key omitted from the map keeps its current value. A field key the object does not define is ignored, and a key sent with a blank value (null or an empty string) clears that field's value. Two exceptions: a read-only field such as a roll-up field is validated rather than cleared, and the standard field keys are recognised here, so sending one with a value returns a validation error instead of being ignored. `null` in place of the whole map is accepted and writes nothing, whereas a field key *inside* the map whose value is `null` clears that field
---@field external_id? string|distilled.Null An id you can use to link the record to your own record keeping. Omitting it, or sending `null`, leaves the current value in place — `null` is accepted but is a no-op, so it cannot be used to clear the external id
---@field name? string|distilled.Null The name of the record. Omitting it, or sending `null`, leaves the current name in place — `null` is accepted but is a no-op, so it cannot be used to clear the name. Rejected if the object's name field has `autoincrement_enabled`, and must be unique if it has `is_unique`
---@field photo? zendesk.CustomObjectRecordUpdateInputPhoto|distilled.Null A photo to attach to the record. Requires `allows_photos` on the custom object. Sending an explicit null removes the current photo, whereas omitting the property leaves it in place. Uploading actual image data requires a `multipart/form-data` request — see the cURL sample

