---@field query string The search query. See [Query basics](#query-basics) above. For details on the query syntax, see the [Zendesk Support search reference](https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408886879258)
---@field sort_by? string One of `updated_at`, `created_at`, `priority`, `status`, or `ticket_type`. Defaults to sorting by relevance
---@field sort_order? string One of `asc` or `desc`. Defaults to `desc`
---@field brand_id? zendesk.ListSearchResultsRequestBrandId When brand separation is enabled, scopes user results to the specified brand. This parameter applies only when the account has brand separation enabled (`brand_user_separation_eap`). Non-user document types are unaffected. Accepted values: * "all" — apply no brand scoping. * 0 — return only account-scoped (brand-less) end users; all agents and admins remain visible. * A numeric brand id — if the brand has user separation enabled, return that brand's end users plus the agents with membership in that brand and all admins. If the brand's users are account-scoped, return the account-scoped end users, any end users still indexed with the brand, and all agents and admins. A brand id that does not exist on the account matches no users. Any other value returns 400 Bad Request, whether or not brand separation is enabled. Omitting `brand_id` applies no brand scoping; this endpoint does not fall back to the account's default brand_id behavior. Use the users search and autocomplete endpoints for that fallback. The query keyword forms (`query=type:user brand_id:{brand_id}` and `brand:{brand_id}`) continue to work. They match the brand id as indexed on every document type, so agents and admins match only through their brand memberships. When brand separation is enabled and the brand's users are account-scoped, they also match every account-scoped end user, and a negated keyword (`-brand_id:{brand_id}`) excludes those end users too.
---@field include? string Sideloads to include in the response. Accepts a comma-separated list of values. The available sideloads depend on the search result types.

