---@class zendesk.OpenUsersProfileInAgentBrowserRequest
---@field agent_id number ID of an agent
---@field user_id number The id of the user

---One organization to update, with the attributes to apply to it. The organization is matched by `id` first, then by `external_id`; `id` wins when both are supplied. An item that names neither, or that names an organization outside the account, is not rejected at the door — it is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure against that item in the job results. Every attribute is optional on a matched organization, and omitting one leaves the stored value unchanged — but see the per-property notes, as several properties replace the stored value wholesale rather than merging into it.
---An agent without edit permission on organizations can only change `notes`; for such an agent every other attribute here is silently ignored and the item still counts as updated. `organization_fields` is the exception — it is applied for a restricted agent too, with no permission gate on this route.
---@class zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInput
---@field details? string|distilled.Null Any details about the organization, such as the address. `null` clears the stored value.
---@field domain_names? zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputDomainNames Domain names and email addresses to associate with this organization. Accepts either an array of values or a single string of values separated by commas, semicolons or spaces. **Destructive:** the supplied list replaces the stored list wholesale, so every omitted domain and email is deleted; `null`, an empty array and an empty string all clear them all. Omit the property entirely to leave the stored list untouched.
---@field external_id? zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputExternalId A unique external id to associate the organization to an external record. Plays two roles in this mode: when `id` is omitted or `null`, the `external_id` string selects the organization to update, and an item that names neither, or names an organization outside the account, is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure, not an HTTP error; and on a matched organization the value is written back as its external id — a no-op when the value is what matched, a change when `id` did the matching. `null` or an empty string clears the stored value when `id` selected the organization; a value already used by another organization is recorded as an update failure for this item.
---@field group_id? number|distilled.Null New tickets from users in this organization are automatically put in this group. `null` unassigns the group.
---@field id? zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputId The id of the organization to update. Accepts an integer or its decimal string form. An unknown id is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure for this item, not an HTTP error. Omit it, or send `null`, to match on `external_id` alone.
---@field name? string A unique name for the organization. Cannot contain the `|` character and must be unique within the account; a duplicate is recorded as an update failure for this item. Cannot be cleared — an empty string or `null` fails the presence validation the same way.
---@field notes? string|distilled.Null Any notes you have about the organization. `null` clears the stored value for an agent with edit permission; for an agent restricted to `edit_notes`, `null` is ignored and the item still reports success with the old notes — send an empty string to clear. This is the only attribute an agent restricted to `edit_notes` can change; for such an agent every other attribute here is silently ignored and the item still counts as updated. `organization_fields` is the exception — it is applied for a restricted agent too, with no permission gate on this route.
---@field organization_fields? zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputOrganizationFieldsMap Custom organization field values, keyed by field key, applied to the matched organization. See [Custom organization fields](/api-reference/ticketing/organizations/organizations/#custom-organization-fields). Only keys matching an existing organization field take effect; unknown keys are silently ignored. Supplied keys are merged into the stored values, so omitted keys keep their value, and a `null` or blank value for a key deletes that field's stored value. Do not send `null` for the whole map — the batch job cannot handle it and the item fails; omit the property or send an empty object instead. Applied with no permission gate on this route — even for an agent restricted to `edit_notes`, and with no `up_restrict_organization_field_updates` check, unlike the single-organization update. Requires the `user_and_organization_fields` account feature; without it the whole map is silently ignored.
---@field shared_comments? boolean End users in this organization are able to comment on each other's tickets. An explicit `null` is rejected with 400. Only takes effect when `shared_tickets` is `true` — the model resets it to `false` whenever the organization is not sharing tickets.
---@field shared_tickets? boolean End users in this organization are able to see each other's tickets. An explicit `null` is rejected with 400. Setting this to `false` also forces `shared_comments` to `false`. If both this and `ticket_restriction` are supplied and they disagree, either may win — this route does not run the single-update precedence resolver — so do not send conflicting values; the stored restriction and sharing state depend on which value actually changed.
---@field tags? zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputTags The tags of the organization. Accepts either an array of tags or a single string of space-separated tags. Requires the `user_and_organization_tags` account feature; without it the value is accepted and silently ignored. **Destructive:** the supplied list replaces the stored list wholesale, so every omitted tag is deleted; `null`, an empty array and an empty string all remove every tag. Omit the property entirely to leave the stored tags untouched.
---@field ticket_restriction? zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputTicketRestriction|string|distilled.Null Who can see the tickets of end users in this organization: `restricted` hides them from each other, `organization` shares them within the organization, and `organization_and_descendants` shares them within the organization and its descendants — the last requires the organization hierarchy feature, and is recorded as an update failure for this item without it. An unknown value is likewise recorded as an update failure rather than rejected at the door. Interacts with `shared_tickets`: if both this and `shared_tickets` are supplied and they disagree, either may win — this route does not run the single-update precedence resolver — so do not send conflicting values; the stored restriction and sharing state depend on which value actually changed. An explicit `null` is accepted and clears the stored restriction value; when ticket-restriction sync is enabled the shared-tickets flag is then re-derived from it, so the organization is treated as sharing (the restriction reads back as `organization`).

---Domain names and email addresses to associate with this organization. Accepts either an array of values or a single string of values separated by commas, semicolons or spaces. **Destructive:** the supplied list replaces the stored list wholesale, so every omitted domain and email is deleted; `null`, an empty array and an empty string all clear them all. Omit the property entirely to leave the stored list untouched.
---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputDomainNames zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputDomainNamesCase0List|string

---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputDomainNamesCase0List string[]

---A unique external id to associate the organization to an external record. Plays two roles in this mode: when `id` is omitted or `null`, the `external_id` string selects the organization to update, and an item that names neither, or names an organization outside the account, is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure, not an HTTP error; and on a matched organization the value is written back as its external id — a no-op when the value is what matched, a change when `id` did the matching. `null` or an empty string clears the stored value when `id` selected the organization; a value already used by another organization is recorded as an update failure for this item.
---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputExternalId string|number

---The id of the organization to update. Accepts an integer or its decimal string form. An unknown id is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure for this item, not an HTTP error. Omit it, or send `null`, to match on `external_id` alone.
---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputId number|string

---Custom organization field values, keyed by field key, applied to the matched organization. See [Custom organization fields](/api-reference/ticketing/organizations/organizations/#custom-organization-fields). Only keys matching an existing organization field take effect; unknown keys are silently ignored. Supplied keys are merged into the stored values, so omitted keys keep their value, and a `null` or blank value for a key deletes that field's stored value. Do not send `null` for the whole map — the batch job cannot handle it and the item fails; omit the property or send an empty object instead. Applied with no permission gate on this route — even for an agent restricted to `edit_notes`, and with no `up_restrict_organization_field_updates` check, unlike the single-organization update. Requires the `user_and_organization_fields` account feature; without it the whole map is silently ignored.
---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputOrganizationFieldsMap { [string]: any }

---The tags of the organization. Accepts either an array of tags or a single string of space-separated tags. Requires the `user_and_organization_tags` account feature; without it the value is accepted and silently ignored. **Destructive:** the supplied list replaces the stored list wholesale, so every omitted tag is deleted; `null`, an empty array and an empty string all remove every tag. Omit the property entirely to leave the stored tags untouched.
---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputTags zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputTagsCase0List|string

---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputTagsCase0List string[]

---Who can see the tickets of end users in this organization: `restricted` hides them from each other, `organization` shares them within the organization, and `organization_and_descendants` shares them within the organization and its descendants — the last requires the organization hierarchy feature, and is recorded as an update failure for this item without it. An unknown value is likewise recorded as an update failure rather than rejected at the door.
---Interacts with `shared_tickets`: if both this and `shared_tickets` are supplied and they disagree, either may win — this route does not run the single-update precedence resolver — so do not send conflicting values; the stored restriction and sharing state depend on which value actually changed.
---An explicit `null` is accepted and clears the stored restriction value; when ticket-restriction sync is enabled the shared-tickets flag is then re-derived from it, so the organization is treated as sharing (the restriction reads back as `organization`).
---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInputTicketRestriction "restricted"|"organization"|"organization_and_descendants"

---Batch-update mode: each item in `organizations` names one organization by `id` or `external_id` and carries the attributes to apply to it, so a single request can make different changes to up to 100 organizations. An empty array, an explicit null and a missing wrapper are all rejected with 400.
---@class zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateRequest
---@field organizations zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateRequestOrganizationsList

---@alias zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateRequestOrganizationsList zendesk.OrganizationBatchUpdateItemInput[]

---The organization attributes to apply to every organization matched by the `ids` or `external_ids` query parameter. Every property is optional and omitting one leaves the stored values unchanged on every matched organization, but see the per-property notes: several properties replace the stored value wholesale rather than merging into it. An empty object is accepted and changes nothing.
---An organization named by the query parameter that does not exist in the account is not rejected at the door — it is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure for that organization in the job results.
---An agent without edit permission on organizations can only change `notes`; for such an agent every other attribute here is silently ignored and the organization still counts as updated. `organization_fields` is the exception — it is applied for a restricted agent too, with no permission gate on this route.
---@class zendesk.OrganizationBulkUpdateInput
---@field details? string|distilled.Null Any details about the organizations, such as the address. `null` clears the stored value.
---@field domain_names? zendesk.OrganizationBulkUpdateInputDomainNames Domain names and email addresses to associate with the organizations. Accepts either an array of values or a single string of values separated by commas, semicolons or spaces. **Destructive:** the supplied list replaces the stored list wholesale on every matched organization, so every omitted domain and email is deleted; `null`, an empty array and an empty string all clear them all. Omit the property entirely to leave the stored lists untouched.
---@field external_id? zendesk.OrganizationBulkUpdateInputExternalId A unique external id to associate the organizations to an external record. **In bulk mode this rewrites the external id of every matched organization**, which is rarely what a bulk update means to do. The id is case-insensitive, so "company1" and "Company1" are considered the same. `null` or an empty string clears it; a value already used by another organization is recorded as an update failure for every organization it was applied to.
---@field group_id? number|distilled.Null New tickets from users in these organizations are automatically put in this group. `null` unassigns the group.
---@field name? string A unique name for the organization. Cannot contain the `|` character and must be unique within the account; a duplicate is recorded as an update failure for every organization it was applied to. Cannot be cleared — an empty string or `null` fails the presence validation the same way.
---@field notes? string|distilled.Null Any notes about the organizations. `null` clears the stored value. This is the only attribute an agent restricted to `edit_notes` can change; for such an agent every other attribute here is silently ignored and the matched organizations still count as updated. `organization_fields` is the exception — it is applied for a restricted agent too, with no permission gate on this route.
---@field organization_fields? zendesk.OrganizationBulkUpdateInputOrganizationFieldsMap|distilled.Null Custom organization field values, keyed by field key, applied to every matched organization. See [Custom organization fields](/api-reference/ticketing/organizations/organizations/#custom-organization-fields). Only keys matching an existing organization field take effect; unknown keys are silently ignored. Supplied keys are merged into the stored values, so omitted keys keep their value, and a `null` or blank value for a key deletes that field's stored value. `null` for the whole map is accepted and changes nothing. Applied with no permission gate on this route — even for an agent restricted to `edit_notes`, and with no `up_restrict_organization_field_updates` check, unlike the single-organization update. Requires the `user_and_organization_fields` account feature; without it the whole map is silently ignored.
---@field shared_comments? boolean End users in these organizations are able to comment on each other's tickets. An explicit `null` is rejected with 400. Only takes effect when `shared_tickets` is `true` — the model resets it to `false` whenever an organization is not sharing tickets.
---@field shared_tickets? boolean End users in these organizations are able to see each other's tickets. An explicit `null` is rejected with 400. Setting this to `false` also forces `shared_comments` to `false`. If both this and `ticket_restriction` are supplied and they disagree, either may win — this route does not run the single-update precedence resolver — so do not send conflicting values; the stored restriction and sharing state depend on which value actually changed.
---@field tags? zendesk.OrganizationBulkUpdateInputTags The tags of the organizations. Accepts either an array of tags or a single string of space-separated tags. Requires the `user_and_organization_tags` account feature; without it the value is accepted and silently ignored. **Destructive:** the supplied list replaces the stored list wholesale on every matched organization, so every omitted tag is deleted; `null`, an empty array and an empty string all remove every tag. Omit the property entirely to leave the stored tags untouched.
---@field ticket_restriction? zendesk.OrganizationBulkUpdateInputTicketRestriction|string|distilled.Null Who can see the tickets of end users in these organizations: `restricted` hides them from each other, `organization` shares them within the organization, and `organization_and_descendants` shares them within the organization and its descendants — the last requires the organization hierarchy feature, and is recorded as an update failure without it. An unknown value is likewise recorded as an update failure rather than rejected at the door. Interacts with `shared_tickets`: if both this and `shared_tickets` are supplied and they disagree, either may win — this route does not run the single-update precedence resolver — so do not send conflicting values; the stored restriction and sharing state depend on which value actually changed. An explicit `null` is accepted and clears the stored restriction value; when ticket-restriction sync is enabled the shared-tickets flag is then re-derived from it, so the organization is treated as sharing (the restriction reads back as `organization`).

