---One membership to create: a user and an organization to assign them to. Neither property is required, and neither is checked at the door — an item that omits or nulls either one, or that names a user or an organization outside the account, is accepted with a 200 and recorded as a `UserNotFound` or `OrganizationNotFound` failure against that item in the job results. An item whose user already belongs to the named organization is not a failure: the existing membership is returned unchanged, so the item is idempotent.
---Creating a membership has effects beyond the new row. On accounts **without** the multiple organizations setting, the same user may appear in at most one item in the whole request — the same user in two items, even two identical items, is rejected with 400 before the job starts — and creating a membership **deletes** the user's other organization memberships. On accounts with the `ocp_tighten_agent_org_access` feature, an item whose user the caller cannot manage organization memberships for is recorded as a `PermissionDenied` failure.
---@class zendesk.OrganizationMembershipCreateManyItemInput
---@field organization_id? number|distilled.Null The ID of the organization to assign the user to. An explicit `null` behaves exactly like an unknown ID: the item is recorded as an `OrganizationNotFound` failure in the job results. Creating a membership has effects beyond the new row: * If this is the user's first organization membership, it is created as the default, which also sets the user's `organization_id`, and every unclosed ticket requested by the user is reassigned to this organization. * On accounts **without** the multiple organizations setting, all of the user's other organization memberships are **deleted**, and the user's unclosed requested tickets that belonged to those removed organizations move to the user's new default organization — or have their organization cleared, if the account has the `unset_ticket_org_when_removing_default_user_org` feature. Tickets the user has in other organizations are left alone. * An item is recorded as a failure if the user is already at the account's `max_organization_memberships_per_user` limit (300 by default).
---@field user_id? number|distilled.Null The ID of the user to assign to the organization. The user must belong to the same account as the organization. An explicit `null` behaves exactly like an unknown ID: the item is recorded as a `UserNotFound` failure in the job results.

---@class zendesk.OrganizationMembershipInput
---@field organization_id? number The ID of the organization to assign the user to. Required **unless** an `organization_id` is supplied at the root of the request or as a query parameter *and* no `user_id` is supplied in either of those places, in which case that value selects the organization and this one is ignored. Creating a membership has effects beyond the new row: * If this is the user's first organization membership, it is created as the default, which also sets the user's `organization_id`, and every unclosed ticket requested by the user is reassigned to this organization. * On accounts **without** the multiple organizations feature, all of the user's other organization memberships are **deleted**, and the user's unclosed requested tickets that belonged to those removed organizations move to the user's new default organization — or have their organization cleared, if the account has the `unset_ticket_org_when_removing_default_user_org` feature. Tickets the user has in other organizations are left alone. * Returns a 422 if the user is already at the account's `max_organization_memberships_per_user` limit (300 by default).
---@field user_id? number The ID of the user to assign to the organization. The user must belong to the same account as the organization. Required **unless** a `user_id` is supplied at the root of the request or as a query parameter, in which case that value selects the user and this one is ignored.

