---The writable attributes accepted when updating a queue. `name`, `description` and `definition` are left unchanged when omitted, but
---`priority` and the group assignments are always rewritten from the request, so a group queue must send `priority` and `primary_groups_id`
---on every update. A subqueue queue omits both instead, and sends `subqueues`
---@class zendesk.QueueUpdateInput
---@field definition? zendesk.QueueUpdateInputDefinition `null` is accepted and leaves the stored definition unchanged
---@field description? string|distilled.Null The description of the queue. `null` clears the stored description; on create it creates a queue with no description
---@field name? string The name of the queue. Must be unique within the account, including against subqueue names
---@field predictive_routing? boolean Whether predictive routing is applied to the queue. Forced to `false` when the queue has subqueues
---@field primary_groups_id? zendesk.QueueUpdateInputPrimaryGroupsIdList|distilled.Null The ids of the groups the queue routes to first. Replaces the current primary groups, so a group queue must send the full list and it cannot be empty. Must be omitted when the queue has subqueues. `null`, an empty array and omitting the property are all the same request: each clears the primary groups, which a group queue then rejects with a 422 that rolls the whole update back, and which is the required state for a subqueue queue
---@field priority? number|distilled.Null The priority applied to tickets that enter the queue. Rewritten on every update, so omitting it on a group queue clears the priority and fails validation. Must be omitted when the queue has subqueues. `null` is indistinguishable from omitting the property — the controller rewrites `priority` from the request either way — so it fails validation on a group queue and is the required value on a subqueue queue
---@field secondary_groups_id? zendesk.QueueUpdateInputSecondaryGroupsIdList|distilled.Null The ids of the groups the queue falls back to. Replaces the current secondary groups, and clears them when omitted. Must be omitted when the queue has subqueues. Ids also present in `primary_groups_id` are ignored. **`null` is destructive and silent**: like an empty array and like omitting the property, it removes every secondary group, and because nothing validates secondary groups the request still returns a 200
---@field subqueues? zendesk.QueueUpdateInputSubqueuesList|distilled.Null The subqueues that tickets are distributed across, instead of routing to groups directly. Applied only when the account has a minimum Enterprise level plan for whichever of Support, Chat, Talk products it has, and ignored without it. On such an account this field is rewritten on every update rather than left alone: the array is the complete set, so any existing subqueue whose id is absent from it is deleted, an empty array deletes them all, and **omitting the field entirely also deletes them all** — which converts the queue to a group queue and so requires `priority` and `primary_groups_id` in the same request. **`null` is destructive in exactly the same way**: the writer tests `sub_queues.nil? || sub_queues.empty?` and takes the delete-everything branch for both, so `null`, `[]` and omission are one request, not three. Pass each subqueue's `id` to keep it, or omit `id` to add a new one

---`null` is accepted and leaves the stored definition unchanged
---@alias zendesk.QueueUpdateInputDefinition zendesk.QueueDefinitionInput|any

---The ids of the groups the queue routes to first. Replaces the current primary groups, so a group queue must send the full list and it
---cannot be empty. Must be omitted when the queue has subqueues. `null`, an empty array and omitting the property are all the same
---request: each clears the primary groups, which a group queue then rejects with a 422 that rolls the whole update back, and which is the
---required state for a subqueue queue
---@alias zendesk.QueueUpdateInputPrimaryGroupsIdList number[]

---The ids of the groups the queue falls back to. Replaces the current secondary groups, and clears them when omitted. Must be omitted
---when the queue has subqueues. Ids also present in `primary_groups_id` are ignored. **`null` is destructive and silent**: like an empty
---array and like omitting the property, it removes every secondary group, and because nothing validates secondary groups the request still
---returns a 200
---@alias zendesk.QueueUpdateInputSecondaryGroupsIdList number[]

---The subqueues that tickets are distributed across, instead of routing to groups directly. Applied only when the account has a
---minimum Enterprise level plan for whichever of Support, Chat, Talk products it has, and ignored without it. On such an account
---this field is rewritten on every update rather than left
---alone: the array is the complete set, so any existing subqueue whose id is absent from it is deleted, an empty array deletes them all,
---and **omitting the field entirely also deletes them all** — which converts the queue to a group queue and so requires `priority` and
---`primary_groups_id` in the same request. **`null` is destructive in exactly the same way**: the writer tests
---`sub_queues.nil? || sub_queues.empty?` and takes the delete-everything branch for both, so `null`, `[]` and omission are one request,
---not three. Pass each subqueue's `id` to keep it, or omit `id` to add a new one
---@alias zendesk.QueueUpdateInputSubqueuesList any[]

