---@class zendesk.RequestEndUserVerificationRequest
---@field user_id number The id of the user
---@field user_identity_id number The ID of the user identity

---The properties an end user may set when creating a request. A request is the end-user view of a ticket, and the set of
---properties accepted here is much narrower than the one accepted by the Tickets API. Properties an end user is not
---allowed to set for the account's configuration are discarded rather than rejected.
---@class zendesk.RequestInput
---@field collaborators? zendesk.RequestInputCollaborators The collaborators (CCs) to set on the request, as user ids, email addresses, or objects carrying a name and an email. Only applied when the account allows collaborators, and rejected when the request is submitted from the help center by an account that does not allow help center collaborators. At most 48 collaborators may be sent in one call on accounts with CCs and followers enabled, and null is ignored
---@field comment zendesk.RequestInputComment
---@field custom_fields? zendesk.RequestInputCustomFields Custom field values, either as an array of objects carrying an `id` and a `value`, or as an object mapping ticket field id to value. Values for fields the end user cannot see are discarded. Takes precedence over `fields` when both are sent. A bare string, integer or boolean is tolerated and ignored, as is null
---@field due_at? string|distilled.Null When a request of type "task" is due. Only applied when the Type field is editable by end users. Send null or an empty string to leave the request without a due date
---@field email_ccs? zendesk.RequestInputEmailCcsList|distilled.Null The email CCs to add to the request, up to 48 entries with `action: "put"` per call. On an account without CCs and followers enabled the `put` entries are still applied, as plain collaborators; only `delete` entries need the feature. Rejected when the request is submitted from the help center by an account that does not allow help center collaborators. Null and an empty array both add no CCs
---@field fields? zendesk.RequestInputFields An alias for `custom_fields`, accepting the same shapes with the same tolerances. Ignored when `custom_fields` is also sent, and null is ignored
---@field metadata? zendesk.RequestInputMetadataMap|distilled.Null An arbitrary object stored as custom metadata on the request's first audit. A value that is not an object, including null, is ignored
---@field organization_id? number|distilled.Null The id of the organization to associate the request with. Must be one of the requester's organizations — any other value, and null, is silently replaced by the requester's default organization
---@field priority? zendesk.RequestInputPriority|string|distilled.Null The priority of the request. Only applied when the Priority field is editable by end users. An unrecognised value, and null, leave the request without a priority
---@field recipient? string The original recipient email address to record on the request
---@field requester? zendesk.RequestInputRequester|distilled.Null The requester of an anonymous request. Only applied when the request is created by an unauthenticated caller — for any authenticated caller the requester is the caller. Null is ignored
---@field safe_update? boolean|distilled.Null Has no effect on its own on create, but see `updated_stamp`: sending both fails the create with a 422
---@field solved? boolean|distilled.Null Accepted for symmetry with Update Request but has no effect on create — a request has no assignee at creation, so it cannot be solved
---@field subject? string The subject of the request. Required when the account requires a subject on end-user requests
---@field system_metadata? zendesk.RequestInputSystemMetadata|distilled.Null Client and IP information to store as system metadata on the request's first audit. Accepted from any caller but only applied for internal Zendesk services, and a null is ignored
---@field tags? zendesk.RequestInputTags The tags to set on the request. Null is ignored and leaves the request with no tags of its own
---@field ticket_form_id? number|distilled.Null The id of the ticket form to apply. Only applied when the account has ticket forms. Sending null behaves exactly like omitting the property — the brand's default form is used
---@field type? zendesk.RequestInputType|string|distilled.Null The type of the request. Only applied when the Type field is editable by end users. An unrecognised value, and null, leave the request without a type
---@field updated_stamp? string|distilled.Null Has no effect on its own — there is no earlier `updated_at` to compare against — but sending it together with `safe_update: true` **fails the create** with a 422, because the safe-update check runs on a record that has no stored timestamp yet. Send neither on create
---@field via? zendesk.RequestInputVia The channel the request came through
---@field via_followup_source_id? number|distilled.Null The id of a closed ticket that this request follows up on. Only closed tickets are accepted; any other value, and null, is ignored
---@field via_id? number|distilled.Null The channel to record the request against. Only a small set of channels may be chosen by an API caller — any other value, and null, records the request as created through the API. Ignored when `via_followup_source_id` is sent, which records the request as a follow-up instead

