---Only the supplied properties are updated. Supplying `policy_metrics` replaces the policy's targets in full. Every property here is also a no-op when sent as null, so no property can be cleared that way
---@class zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInput
---@field description? string|distilled.Null The description of the SLA policy. A blank or `null` value is ignored, so the description cannot be cleared through this endpoint
---@field filter? zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputFilter The conditions a ticket must match for the SLA policy to be applied to it. A supplied filter **replaces the policy's conditions in full**, so omitting `all` or sending it as `null` or `[]` removes the policy's existing `all` conditions - and likewise for `any`. Sending `null` or `{}` for `filter` itself is a no-op that leaves the current filter in place
---@field metric_settings? zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputMetricSettings Per-metric activation and fulfillment settings. `null` is ignored and leaves the current settings in place, whereas `{}` **clears every setting** on the policy
---@field policy_metrics? zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputPolicyMetricsList|distilled.Null The targets for the policy. See [Policy Metric](#policy-metric). Supplying this replaces the policy's targets in full, so a partial list deletes the targets it omits. `null` and `[]` are both ignored and leave the current targets in place
---@field policy_triggers? zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputPolicyTriggersList|distilled.Null Notifications attached to the policy. Only acted on when the account has the SLA policy triggers feature; without it the field is accepted and then silently dropped. When it is acted on, the supplied list replaces the policy's notifications in full. `null` and `[]` are both ignored and leave the current notifications in place
---@field position? number|distilled.Null Position of the SLA policy, which determines the order policies are matched in. A `null` value is ignored and the current position is kept
---@field title? string|distilled.Null The title of the SLA policy. A blank or `null` value is ignored and the current title is kept

---The conditions a ticket must match for the SLA policy to be applied to it. A supplied filter **replaces the policy's conditions in full**, so omitting `all` or sending it as `null` or `[]` removes the policy's existing `all` conditions - and likewise for `any`. Sending `null` or `{}` for `filter` itself is a no-op that leaves the current filter in place
---@alias zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputFilter zendesk.SLAPolicyFilterInput|any

---Per-metric activation and fulfillment settings. `null` is ignored and leaves the current settings in place, whereas `{}` **clears every setting** on the policy
---@alias zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputMetricSettings zendesk.SLAPolicyMetricSettingsInput|any

---The targets for the policy. See [Policy Metric](#policy-metric). Supplying this replaces the policy's targets in full, so a partial list deletes the targets it omits. `null` and `[]` are both ignored and leave the current targets in place
---@alias zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputPolicyMetricsList zendesk.SLAPolicyMetricInput[]

---Notifications attached to the policy. Only acted on when the account has the SLA policy triggers feature; without it the field is accepted and then silently dropped. When it is acted on, the supplied list replaces the policy's notifications in full. `null` and `[]` are both ignored and leave the current notifications in place
---@alias zendesk.SLAPolicyUpdateInputPolicyTriggersList zendesk.SLAPolicyTriggerInput[]

---@class zendesk.SatisfactionRatingInput
---@field comment? string|distilled.Null The comment left by the requester with this rating. Omitting the property, sending an empty string and sending `null` are equivalent — the rating is created with no comment. A non-empty comment upgrades the score stored on the ticket from "good" to "good_with_comment" or from "bad" to "bad_with_comment", although the `score` in the response still reads "good" or "bad"
---@field reason_code? number|distilled.Null The reason code for a negative rating. See [Reason codes](/api-reference/ticketing/ticket-management/satisfaction_reasons/#reason-codes) in the Satisfaction Reasons API. **This property is only accepted on accounts that have satisfaction reasons enabled**; on any other account the property is silently dropped and the rating is created without it, with a success response and no error. It also only takes effect on a "bad" score — a "good" score resets the reason to the default 0 ("no reason"). An explicit `null` is accepted and ignored, preserving any reason code already recorded on the ticket
---@field score zendesk.SatisfactionRatingInputScore|string The rating. Only "good" and "bad" create a rating: the parameter filter accepts the full satisfaction-score vocabulary used by the List Satisfaction Ratings `score` filter ("offered", "unoffered", "received", "good_with_comment" and so on), but any value other than "good" or "bad" is mapped to no score and the request then fails validation with a 422 rather than a 400

---The rating. Only "good" and "bad" create a rating: the parameter filter accepts the full satisfaction-score vocabulary used by the List Satisfaction Ratings `score` filter ("offered", "unoffered", "received", "good_with_comment" and so on), but any value other than "good" or "bad" is mapped to no score and the request then fails validation with a 422 rather than a 400
---@alias zendesk.SatisfactionRatingInputScore "good"|"bad"

---@class zendesk.SatisfactionRatingObject
