---@field partner_name? string|distilled.Null Can be one of the following: "jira", null
---@field remote_subdomain? string Subdomain of the remote account or null if not associated with an account
---@field status? string Can be one of the following: "accepted", "declined", "pending", "inactive", "failed", "ssl_error", "configuration_error"
---@field type? string Can be one of the following: "inbound", "outbound"
---@field updated_at? string The time the record was updated
---@field url? string URL of the sharing agreement record

---@class zendesk.SharingAgreementResponse
---@field sharing_agreement? zendesk.SharingAgreementObject

---The writable attributes accepted when updating a sharing agreement. Only `status` can be changed;
---the sharing options chosen when the agreement was created are fixed for its lifetime. Sending an
---empty object is accepted and changes nothing, though the agreement is re-validated on every request,
---so the rejections described under `status` can apply even to a request that changes nothing.
---@class zendesk.SharingAgreementUpdateInput
---@field status? zendesk.SharingAgreementUpdateInputStatus|string|distilled.Null The state of the agreement. Use `accepted` to accept an invitation you have received, `declined` to reject or cancel one, `inactive` to suspend an accepted agreement without deleting it, and `accepted` again to resume a suspended one. A request that actually changes the status sends the new state to the account on the other side of the agreement, unless the new status is `ssl_error`; a request that leaves the status as it was sends nothing. Moving to `inactive`, `failed`, `ssl_error` or `configuration_error` also emails every administrator on your account; the other statuses do not. Three transitions are rejected. Moving back to `accepted` is rejected unless Zendesk recorded your own account as the one that suspended the agreement, which it does only for an `accepted` to `inactive` change made on your side — so an agreement the *other* account suspended cannot be resumed from here. Moving to `pending` or `accepted` is rejected while you hold another accepted agreement with the same remote URL in the same direction. Moving to anything other than `inactive` or `declined` is rejected when the agreement's remote URL has come to point back at your own account, which can happen after a subdomain change. `failed`, `ssl_error` and `configuration_error` describe communication problems and are normally set by Zendesk rather than by a client, but they are accepted here. Note that `declined` set through this endpoint marks the agreement declined without deleting it or its shared tickets — use the delete endpoint for that. A blank value is accepted and leaves the status unchanged. `null` and an empty string are both listed in the enum for that reason; a whitespace-only string such as `" "` behaves the same way but cannot be enumerated. Omitting the property has the same effect. An unrecognized status name is rejected.

---The state of the agreement. Use `accepted` to accept an invitation you have received, `declined`
---to reject or cancel one, `inactive` to suspend an accepted agreement without deleting it, and
---`accepted` again to resume a suspended one. A request that actually changes the status sends the
---new state to the account on the other side of the agreement, unless the new status is
---`ssl_error`; a request that leaves the status as it was sends nothing. Moving to `inactive`,
---`failed`, `ssl_error` or `configuration_error` also emails every administrator on your account;
---the other statuses do not.
