---@class zendesk.UserFieldResponse
---@field user_field? zendesk.UserFieldObject

---The writable attributes accepted when updating a user field. The field's `type`, `key`, and
---`relationship_target_type` cannot be changed after creation, so none of them is listed here. `type` and
---`relationship_target_type` are discarded if sent; sending a `key` different from the field's current key fails
---validation.
---@class zendesk.UserFieldUpdateInput
---@field active? boolean|distilled.Null Whether this field is available. Sending null does not leave the field unchanged: it reactivates the field, because a nil `is_active` is defaulted back to `true`.
---@field custom_field_options? zendesk.UserFieldUpdateInputCustomFieldOptionsList|distilled.Null For `dropdown`, `tagger`, and `multiselect` fields only. Sending it for any other field type fails with an `Invalid` error on the request. **Destructive:** this array is a full replacement, not a patch. Every existing option you want to keep must be present in every update request. Any option you omit is deleted, and those values are removed from the users that held them. Passing an empty array deletes every option on the field. Omitting the `custom_field_options` key entirely leaves the options untouched, so a partial update must omit the key rather than send a partial list. Every entry must include an `id`: supply an existing option's `id` to update it in place, or a null or empty `id` to add a new option. An entry with no `id` key at all is rejected. The array order determines each option's position. Null and `[]` are **not** interchangeable here. `custom_field_options: null` is accepted and ignored, leaving the options untouched — it is the same as omitting the key. `custom_field_options: []` deletes every option on the field and returns a 200. Send null, or omit the key, if you mean "leave the options alone".
---@field description? string|distilled.Null User-defined description of this field's purpose. Sending null does not clear it: the field's current `title` is written into `description` instead.
---@field permissions? zendesk.UserFieldPermissionsInput|distilled.Null
---@field position? number Ordering of the field relative to other fields. Unlike on create, this writes the field's position directly without shifting its siblings, so it can leave two fields sharing a position. Use Reorder User Field to reposition a field within the account's ordering.
---@field raw_description? string|distilled.Null The dynamic content placeholder for the description. See [Dynamic Content](/api-reference/ticketing/ticket-management/dynamic_content/). When supplied, this value is written to `description` and any `description` sent alongside it is discarded. Sending null is accepted and discarded: `description` is left as sent, or unchanged if no `description` was sent.
---@field raw_title? string|distilled.Null The dynamic content placeholder for the title. See [Dynamic Content](/api-reference/ticketing/ticket-management/dynamic_content/). When supplied, this value is written to `title` and any `title` sent alongside it is discarded. Sending null is accepted and discarded: `title` is left as sent, or unchanged if no `title` was sent.
---@field regexp_for_validation? string For `regexp` fields only. The validation pattern for a field value to be deemed valid
---@field relationship_filter? zendesk.UserFieldUpdateInputRelationshipFilter|distilled.Null For `lookup` fields only. A filter definition that limits which records the field's autocomplete returns. Sending null is accepted and ignored, leaving the existing filter untouched; send `{}` to clear it.
---@field tag? string|distilled.Null For `checkbox` fields only. A tag added to the user when the checkbox field is selected. Supplying `tag` for any other field type fails validation. Values are downcased by the API. Sending null is accepted and clears the tag.
---@field title? string The title of the user field

