---@class zendesk.UserIdentityCreateInput
---@field auto_ownership? boolean|distilled.Null When true, creates the email identity as "owned", or as "shared" if another user owns the address. See [Shared email](/api-reference/ticketing/users/user_identities/#shared-email).
---@field ownership? zendesk.UserIdentityCreateInputOwnership|string|distilled.Null Sets the email identity to "owned" or "shared". Defaults to "owned". See [Shared email](/api-reference/ticketing/users/user_identities/#shared-email).
---@field primary? boolean|distilled.Null Makes the new identity the primary one. null is accepted and ignored. Only takes effect for `email` identities. For a verified email identity (`verified: true` or `verification_method: low`) the identity becomes primary immediately; for an unverified one the promotion is deferred - the verification email link carries `make_primary=true`, so the identity becomes primary (and the former primary is removed) when the recipient verifies. Ignored for non-email identity types, including `google`. To promote an identity that already exists, use the [Make Identity Primary](#make-identity-primary) endpoint instead.
---@field skip_verify_email? boolean|distilled.Null Suppresses the verification or welcome email for the new identity. null is accepted and treated exactly like `false`. Only effective when an agent creates an identity for a different user - it is ignored when adding an identity to your own profile.
---@field type zendesk.UserIdentityCreateInputType|string The type of identity to create. Only the types listed here can be created through this endpoint. The remaining identity types (`any_channel`, `foreign`, `sdk`, `microsoft`) are created by internal systems only, and a request for one of them is rejected with a `400`. The deprecated `phone` alias is likewise rejected here - use `phone_number`. `messaging` and `slack` back internal channel integrations. `slack` additionally requires the Slack identity feature to be enabled on the account.
---@field value string The identifier for this identity. An email address for `email` and `google`, the screen name for `twitter`, the Facebook user id for `facebook`, and an E.164 phone number for `phone_number` and `agent_forwarding`. Phone numbers are validated unless the account has phone number validation turned off.
---@field verification_method? zendesk.UserIdentityCreateInputVerificationMethod|string|distilled.Null The verification state to create the identity with. `low` creates a verified identity and suppresses the verification email. Only `none` and `low` are accepted here; the higher methods (`sso`, `embed`, `full`) are set by the corresponding authentication flows. Has no effect for `phone_number` and `agent_forwarding` identities, which are always verified, nor for `slack`. Also ignored when creating an email identity (including `google`) for the account owner: that identity is always created unverified and a verification email is sent. null is accepted and treated exactly like `none`. Because this property wins over `verified`, sending `verified: true` alongside `verification_method: null` creates an unverified identity.
---@field verified? boolean|distilled.Null Creates the identity already verified, equivalent to `verification_method: low`. Deprecated - use `verification_method` instead. Ignored when `verification_method` is also sent - that property wins. null is accepted and treated exactly like `false`. Has no effect for `phone_number` and `agent_forwarding` identities, which are always verified, nor for `slack`. Also ignored when creating an email identity (including `google`) for the account owner: that identity is always created unverified and a verification email is sent.

